1. Introduction
Early differentiation between progressive supranuclear palsy (PSP) and idiopathic Parkinson’s disease (PD) is challenging because the two disorders share many motor features [1], [2], [3]. PSP is a relatively rare condition, affecting about 5–6 people per 100,000 [4] and most clinicians encounter few cases in practice. This limited exposure often delays recognition and contributes to frequent misdiagnosis as PD. Early and accurate diagnosis is essential because PSP and PD differ in progression, prognosis, and treatment strategies [5]. Importantly, misclassification is particularly common in early disease stages when clinical signs are subtle and overlapping [2], highlighting the need for objective and sensitive biomarkers to support differential diagnosis.
Gait and balance impairment are hallmark features of PSP, particularly in the Richardson syndrome phenotype, which presents with early postural instability, frequent backward falls, and vertical gaze palsy [2], [6], [7]. Quantitative gait analyses show that, compared with PD, individuals with PSP walk more slowly, take shorter steps, adopt a wider base of support, and exhibit greater mediolateral sway with reduced trunk rotation [8], [9], [10]. By contrast, early PD often manifests with subtler gait changes, including loss of arm swing, increased stride-to-stride variability, and impaired turning, which may precede clinical diagnosis by years [11], [12]. These partially overlapping yet distinct gait characteristics suggest that quantitative gait assessment may provide a valuable avenue for differential diagnosis.
Wearable inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensors allow high-resolution capture of multi-segment gait kinematics in laboratory and real-world settings [13], [14], [15], [16]. These sensors produce rich, high-dimensional time series that go beyond the capabilities of conventional aggregate metrics. Machine learning (ML) provides complementary advantages by identifying complex, subtle, and potentially non-linear patterns within these multi-sensor signals, facilitating the identification of digital gait biomarkers, while still allowing analyses based on summary features when interpretability is a priority [17], [18], [19], [20].
To our knowledge, only one prior study has applied ML to wearable sensor-based gait data to directly distinguish PSP from PD, reporting ∼86% sensitivity and ∼90% specificity using a six-sensor array [21]. While encouraging, that study relied exclusively on a single logistic regression model trained on pre-processed spatiotemporal gait features. Thus, it remains unclear whether more advanced and interpretable modeling approaches can improve classification while preserving clinical relevance. Relying on a single algorithm leaves unanswered whether multi-layer tree-based ensembles such as eXtreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost) or random forest ML approaches might better capture the complex, non-linear gait signatures distinguishing PSP from PD or address class imbalance more effectively. Ensemble methods are particularly well-suited for wearable-sensor data because they can model high-dimensional, correlated time-series inputs, reduce overfitting by aggregating many decision trees, and naturally handle noisy or imbalanced datasets [22]. Prior work in PD and related movement disorders has shown that ensemble classifiers often outperform linear models by capturing subtle, non-linear interactions among gait variables [11], [12], [17], [18], [19], [20].
Another limitation of prior work is the exclusive reliance on aggregate spatiotemporal gait features to differentiate PD from PSP, without examining raw sensor data [21]. Aggregates summarize gait cycles but attenuate fine-scale information, whereas raw IMU signals preserve intra-cycle transients, cross-segment phase relationships, and frequency-domain/non-linear dynamics [23]. These high-dimensional signatures may be particularly relevant for capturing PSP’s hallmark axial and postural instability, which are not fully represented in summary features. However, the relative performance and clinical interpretability of raw versus aggregate representations in this context remain insufficiently characterized.
Therefore, the present study had two primary aims: (1) to evaluate whether IMU-derived gait measures can distinguish PSP from PD using machine learning (ML), and (2) to compare classification performance between raw time-series signals and aggregate spatiotemporal variables. We further aim to identify clinically interpretable gait features that differentiate PSP from PD, linking data-driven analysis to clinically meaningful outcomes.
2. Methods
2.1. Participants and study design
We conducted a single-center, retrospective observational study using de-identified records from the Parkinson’s Disease and Movement Disorder Clinic at the University of Kansas Medical Center (KUMC). Participants were enrolled in the study between January 2016 and March 2024. From the full clinic database (n = 954), eligible cases were adults with probable PSP-Richardson syndrome [2] and individuals with idiopathic PD diagnosed according to the Movement Disorders Society clinical diagnostic criteria [24] who completed standardized wearable-sensor gait assessments during their clinic visit. Exclusion criteria were applied to the full screened cohort prior to matching. Specifically, we excluded individuals with prior deep brain stimulation surgery (n = 382), who were unable to walk 10 m without physical assistance due to comorbid neurological or orthopedic conditions affecting gait (n = 87), and those with corrupted or incomplete sensor files (n = 41). Subsequently, 444 participants were included for analysis (410 PD, 34 PSP).
Participants with PSP did not differ from those with PD with respect to age, sex, or Hoehn and Yahr (ON) stage. However, disease duration was shorter in PSP than PD. PSP also showed lower MoCA scores, lower LEDD, and more frequent recent falls than PD (Table 1).

