2.2. Protocol and materials
Participants wore six wireless inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensors (Opal v2; APDM Wearable Technologies, Portland, OR, USA [16]). Sensors were positioned bilaterally on the dorsum of each foot over the metatarsus and on the anteromedial aspect of each shank just below the tibial tuberosity, with additional units secured at the sternum and over the lumbar spine at the L5 level (Fig. 1). Each sensor was affixed using adjustable elastic Velcro™ straps to ensure a stable yet comfortable fit. All assessments were performed with participants wearing a safety gait belt, with an examiner providing standby assistance throughout. Each Opal device integrates two tri-axial accelerometers (± 16 g and ± 200 g ranges; 14-bit and 17.5-bit resolutions), a tri-axial gyroscope (± 2000°/s range; 16-bit resolution), and a tri-axial magnetometer (± 8 Gauss range; 12-bit resolution). Data from all channels were sampled at 128 Hz, hardware-synchronized across sensors, and maintained with inter-unit clock drift under 1 ms.
