2.3. Instrumented Timed-Up-and-Go (TUG) protocol
Following a 30-s seated rest to stabilize baseline recordings, participants were instructed to complete the instrumented Timed-Up-and-Go (TUG): (1) Rise from a 45 cm armless chair on an auditory cue; (2) Walk forward 3 m at a self-selected comfortable pace; (3) Execute a 180° turn at a marked location indicated by coloured floor tape, using a self-selected turning strategy (no physical object was circumnavigated); (4) Return to the starting chair; and (5) Sit down in a controlled manner (Fig. 1).
The instrumented Timed Up and Go (iTUG) was the standardized mobility assessment routinely administered with body-worn inertial sensors during clinical visits to the Parkinson's Disease and Movement Disorder Clinic at the University of Kansas Medical Center between 2016 and 2024. The iTUG is particularly relevant for the study of progressive supranuclear palsy (PSP) because it provides an integrated assessment of functional mobility within a single brief, validated task. Specifically, it captures sit-to-stand, gait initiation, steady-state walking, a 180° turn, and turn-to-sit, thereby sampling motor domains commonly affected in PSP, including gait initiation, axial motor control, turning, and postural transitions. In addition, the iTUG is practical for routine clinical use and can be performed safely by individuals with early postural instability and increased fall risk, making it a clinically relevant measure of mobility challenges encountered in daily activities.
Two trials were collected, and the trial with the fewest data packet losses (< 0.1% of total samples) was retained for analysis. The instrumented TUG has been shown to provide reliable and valid measures of mobility and postural control in Parkinsonian disorders (intraclass correlation coefficient > 0.90) [15], [16].
2.4. Data analysis
2.4.1. Comparison of group differences
Group differences in demographic and clinical variables were assessed using independent-samples t-tests for continuous variables with normal distributions, and Mann–Whitney U tests otherwise. Categorical variables were compared using Chi-square or Fisher’s exact tests, as appropriate, where expected cell counts were < 5. A two-tailed significance threshold of p < 0.05 was applied.
2.5. Raw data set
The raw data set consisted of the synchronized IMU time-series (HDF5 files) from all six sensors (tri-axial accelerometer, gyroscope, and magnetometer channels, 128 Hz). Raw channels were z-standardized per subject and sensor within the training folds, and the resulting parameters (mean, SD) were then applied to the corresponding test folds to avoid data leakage.
Because individual instrumented TUG trials differed in duration and number of strides, the multichannel signals were of variable length and were transformed into fixed-dimensional inputs prior to classification. Each of the 54 channels was resampled to a common temporal length of 512 samples using linear interpolation, thereby preserving the temporal morphology of the waveform while normalizing trial duration across participants. The resampled channels were subsequently concatenated into a single fixed-length vector (54 × 512 = 27,648 values) for each participant, which served as the input to the machine-learning classifiers. No sliding-window segmentation or time-domain feature engineering was applied to the raw modality. All preprocessing parameters were fixed a priori and applied identically to both groups, and normalization statistics were estimated exclusively from the training folds to prevent data leakage.
2.6. Aggregate, pre-processed data set
A total of 130 spatiotemporal gait and postural stability metrics were extracted automatically using the Mobility Lab software (APDM, Inc., Portland, OR, USA) [16], [25]. The software’s pre-processed outputs have been validated by comparison with measurements from a 3-dimensional motion capture system, demonstrating high accuracy [25], [26].
From these, 48 variables with strong clinical applicability were retained for analysis, selected according to (1) a published review highlighting gait and balance parameters most relevant in neurological disorders [23] and (2) the clinical expertise of the study team [27] (Table 2; Supplementary Table 1). Detailed descriptions and graphical examples of each metric are publicly available on the manufacturer’s website (https://www.apdm.com/mobility/).

