2.7. Addressing imbalance in class distribution
The class distribution was notably imbalanced, with 410 participants diagnosed with PD (∼92.4%) and 34 with PSP (∼7.6%). To address class imbalance, we applied the Synthetic Minority Over-sampling Technique (SMOTE) [28] exclusively within the training folds, with all resampling performed only on the training data. SMOTE was never applied to the held-out test folds. Because SMOTE can have limited benefit with some classifiers (e.g., tree-based ensembles), we also compared models with and without resampling as a sensitivity check, which produced similar results (Supplementary Table 5). Missing data accounted for only 0.0002% of all feature values. Missing values were rare (<0.001% per feature) and were imputed within the training folds only, using mean (for continuous variables) or mode (for categorical variables) imputation estimated from the training data distribution. The same parameters were then applied to the held-out test folds to avoid data leakage. As a sensitivity check, we repeated analyses using a multivariate imputation approach incorporating all available features; results were consistent. Specifically, within each cross-validation iteration, preprocessing was performed in the following order: (1) missing-data imputation, (2) feature standardization, and (3) SMOTE applied exclusively to the training fold. For the corresponding held-out test fold, only the imputation and standardization parameters estimated from the training fold were applied.
2.8. Classification approach and model selection
We compared eight supervised classifiers: logistic regression (LR), support vector machine (SVM), k-nearest neighbors (kNN), neural network (NN; MLP), decision tree (DT), random forest (RF), extreme gradient boosting (XGBoost), and a reference “dummy” classifier that always predicts the majority class. To ensure stable optimization, features were z-score standardized within the training folds for models sensitive to feature scale (SVM, kNN) and for linear/logistic models and neural networks where standardization can improve convergence. For scale-invariant tree-based models (DT, RF, XGBoost), raw feature scales were retained. Standardization parameters were estimated from the training data and applied to the held-out folds to avoid data leakage.
We used stratified threefold nested cross-validation, with outer folds reserved for unbiased performance estimation and inner folds used for hyperparameter tuning via grid search (Supplementary Table 2). This ensured that tuning and evaluation were performed on disjoint data to avoid optimistic bias.
A stratified three-fold design was selected a priori because of the limited number of participants with PSP (n = 34). Increasing the number of folds would substantially reduce the number of minority-class samples available in each held-out test fold (approximately seven with five-fold cross-validation and three to four with ten-fold cross-validation), thereby increasing the variability of class-specific performance metrics such as precision, recall, and F1-score. The three-fold design therefore represented an appropriate balance between preserving adequate minority-class representation in each test fold and obtaining an unbiased estimate of model generalization performance. The same hyperparameter ranges were applied for both the aggregate feature dataset and the raw data models. The cross-validation preserved the PSP/PD ratio in each fold, and the final model for each algorithm was refit on all data in the outer training folds using the best hyper-parameters. All classifiers were trained and evaluated twice: once using the raw IMU time-series modality and once using the 48 aggregate features, under an identical cross-validation and tuning protocol.
2.9. Performance evaluation
Model performance was evaluated using multiple classification metrics implemented in scikit-learn (version 1.3.2). The primary model-selection criterion was the macro-averaged F1-score (macro-F1) computed under stratified 3-fold cross-validation. Macro-averaging ensures that both minority (PSP) and majority (PD) classes are weighted equally despite class imbalance. The F1-score is preferred over accuracy as F1 penalizes false positives and false negatives symmetrically and is therefore less biased by class prevalence. We report macro-F1 (each class weighted equally), alongside class-specific F1-scores for PSP and PD. The F1-score (harmonic mean of precision and recall) and additional classifiers such as accuracy (proportion of correct predictions), recall (sensitivity; proportion of true positives identified), and precision (positive predictive value) were calculated using the following formulas (with PSP as positive class):
