3.2. Classification using aggregate features
Fig. 2B presents performance metrics of the ML models on aggregate features, also ranked by macro-F1 score. Similar to the raw time-series models, XGBoost achieved the highest performance (macro-F1 = 0.88), followed by RF (macro-F1 = 0.87). SVM (RBF) ranked next (macro-F1 = 0.84), followed by logistic regression (macro-F1 = 0.83), MLP (macro-F1 = 0.82), kNN (macro-F1 = 0.79), and a single decision tree (macro-F1 = 0.75). As expected for a majority-class baseline, the dummy classifier produced a low macro-F1 score (0.33), reflecting poor sensitivity to the minority (PSP) class.
Other performance measures followed a similar pattern (Fig. 2B; Supplementary Table 4). Class-specific F1-scores showed clear asymmetry (Supplementary Table 4). The top performing XGBoost model exhibited a PSP F1 of 0.81 and a PD F1 of 0.95.
A pooled confusion matrix for the best-performing model (XGBoost), constructed by concatenating the held-out outer-fold predictions, is provided in Supplementary Fig. 1; 27 of the 34 participants with PSP were correctly identified and 7 were classified as PD.
Performance differences between raw and aggregate pipelines were minimal. For example, XGBoost showed only a marginal improvement with aggregate features (macro-F1: 0.87 → 0.88), with similar small differences observed for RF and SVM. As a sensitivity analysis, repeating the aggregate-feature models without SMOTE produced nearly identical classifier rankings and only minimal changes in macro-F1 scores (Δ = 0.00–0.02 for all models; Supplementary Table 5), indicating that the principal findings were not driven by synthetic oversampling.
3.3. Interpretability of aggregate-feature models
To identify gait signatures that drove model decisions, we examined feature importance from the two ensemble classifiers (XGBoost and RF) and computed global SHAP values for these models on the aggregate-feature modality. We focused on these ensembles because they achieved the highest predictive performance in our study, provide native feature-importance measures, and are well-suited for SHAP analysis.
The native feature importance and SHAP values converged on a consistent feature profile: temporal stability and base-of-support measures (e.g., double support [%GC], terminal double support, single-limb support) and spatiotemporal amplitude metrics (e.g., stride length, gait speed) ranked highest, alongside segmental range-of-motion features from the trunk/lumbar segments and mediolateral sway indices (Fig. 3).
