Fig. 3 shows the SHAP summary plot of the top-performing model (XGBoost). The top 12 features were retained for visualization clarity, as feature importance plateaued beyond this rank. Directionality is conveyed by the color coding of individual points (red = higher feature values, blue = lower feature values) and their position on the x-axis (positive SHAP values shift predictions toward PSP, negative values toward PD).
For example, higher values of double support (red points on the positive SHAP axis) increased the likelihood of PSP classification, whereas lower gait speed (blue points on the negative SHAP axis) also shifted predictions toward PSP. Collectively, increased double support and mediolateral sway, together with reduced stride length, gait speed, and trunk/lumbar range of motion, consistently contributed to PSP classification.
We repeated the SHAP analyses using RF. The most influential features in the RF model showed substantial overlap with those identified in the XGBoost model. While absolute SHAP values differed slightly between models, feature rankings and directional effects were largely consistent, supporting the robustness of the identified gait signatures.
The most influential discriminative features originated from multiple locations within the six-IMU array. Spatiotemporal gait measures were derived primarily from the bilateral foot and shank sensors, lumbar range-of-motion and postural sway measures from the lumbar (L5) sensor, and trunk range-of-motion measures from the sternal sensor (Supplementary Table 6). For transparency, performance metrics recomputed from pooled outer-fold predictions are summarized in Supplementary Table S7.
4. Discussion
Using six synchronized IMUs and a prespecified machine-learning pipeline, we show that objective gait information can differentiate PSP from PD, with tree-based ensembles (XGBoost, RF) achieving the strongest cross-validated performance on both raw input and aggregate modalities. XGBoost reached macro-F1 of 0.87 on raw time-series and 0.88 on aggregate data.
Our study adds a head-to-head comparison of raw time-series versus aggregate spatiotemporal features in the same cohort, showing near-equivalent accuracy across modalities, with aggregate features slightly outperforming raw time-series, although differences were minimal, suggesting that input representation may be less critical than model class in this context. This near-equivalent performance was not unexpected. Aggregate spatiotemporal features such as gait speed, stride length, and double support are derived directly from the raw IMU signals, meaning they already capture much of the clinically relevant variance for distinguishing PSP from PD. Prior studies have shown that summary gait metrics retain high discriminative power, with accuracies ≈ 0.89–0.92 in early PSP versus PD [29]. Moreover, machine-learning work in related neurodegenerative cohorts suggests that carefully engineered features can perform as well as, or better than, models trained directly on raw signals, particularly when sample sizes are modest [11], [12], [17], [18], [19], [20].
One possible explanation for the comparable performance of the aggregate-feature models is that the curated representation provided a substantially lower-dimensional input space (48 features versus 27,648 raw signal values per participant), potentially reducing susceptibility to overfitting at the available sample size while preserving clinically informative gait characteristics. These findings support a pragmatic clinical pathway, whereby clinics equipped with systems such as APDM Mobility Lab can implement interpretable models directly from aggregate features without loss of performance relative to raw-signal models in this cohort [16], [25].
Tree-based ensembles consistently outperformed linear, kernel, single-tree, and shallow neural models. This superiority likely reflects their capacity to capture nonlinear interactions and higher-order dependencies among gait features, while remaining robust to noise and multicollinearity [22]. Notably, the most influential features identified by these models, including temporal stability and base-of-support measures (e.g., double support, stance variability, mediolateral sway), align closely with PSP’s characteristic axial and postural instability phenotype [2].
Global SHAP analysis on the aggregate-feature models converged on a coherent clinical picture: more double support and terminal double support, greater mediolateral sway, and reduced stride length, gait speed, and trunk/lumbar ROM pushed predictions toward PSP. These signatures are compatible with PSP’s early postural instability and axial involvement, and with PD’s comparatively preserved base of support early in the disease course [2], [6], [8], [9]. The strong overlap in the top features between XGBoost and RF supports the stability of these signatures across algorithms.
Although turning impairment is a hallmark clinical feature of PSP, the curated aggregate-feature set used in the present study was primarily designed to characterize steady-state gait, axial range of motion, and postural sway, and did not include dedicated turn-specific kinematic variables such as turn duration, number of turn steps, or peak turning velocity. Consequently, the SHAP analysis ranked the most informative features among those available to the models rather than all potentially relevant gait characteristics. Importantly, feature importance should not be interpreted as equivalent to diagnostic specificity. Turn-specific measures may ultimately prove more specific for PSP, even if they were not represented in the present feature set. Future studies incorporating dedicated turning metrics and turn-focused protocols will help determine their incremental diagnostic value for differentiating PSP from PD.
Baseline comparison (Table 1) showed that the PSP and PD groups were well matched for age, sex, and Hoehn & Yahr stage assessed in the ON medication state. The observed differences in disease duration, MoCA score, and LEDD are consistent with the established clinical phenotype of PSP rather than unexpected group imbalances. PSP is characterized by more rapid disease progression, earlier cognitive impairment, and reduced responsiveness to levodopa, resulting in comparable motor severity despite a shorter disease duration and typically lower levodopa-equivalent doses. Because all instrumented assessments were performed in the ON state and the groups were comparable with respect to Hoehn & Yahr stage, these baseline differences are unlikely to fully explain the observed gait differences. Furthermore, the discriminative gait signatures identified by the models, including increased double-support time and mediolateral sway together with reduced stride length, gait speed, and trunk/lumbar range of motion, all closely correspond to the established axial and postural phenotype of PSP. Nevertheless, residual confounding cannot be completely excluded in a retrospective observational study, and future studies using prospectively matched cohorts or covariate-adjusted analyses will be important to further confirm the independence of these gait biomarkers.
Previous quantitative gait models separated early PSP from newly diagnosed PD with overall accuracies ≈ 0.89–0.92, typically showing higher sensitivity for PD than PSP [9]. This pattern mirrors our class-specific F1 asymmetry (Tables S2–S3). In our imbalanced cohort, PSP was harder for the models to detect, and the class-specific F1 for PSP remained lower than for PD even after we applied SMOTE during training, reflecting residual recall asymmetry. More broadly, wearable studies applying ML models attempting multi-disorder differentials tend to achieve lower performance, underscoring the value of a focused PSP–PD design using rich gait signals, which can achieve strong classification performance (macro-F1 ≈ 0.80–0.90) [30]. These findings suggest that clinic-based IMU protocols (e.g., instrumented TUG) can reveal disease-specific gait signatures not captured by standard clinical rating scales, particularly in early disease stages when PSP may resemble PD. However, improving sensitivity for PSP detection remains a key priority, and future work should explore combining gait with complementary modalities such as oculomotor or speech assessments.
Our findings extend the emerging literature on PSP–PD discrimination using wearable sensing and machine learning. Prior differential-diagnosis studies outside direct PSP–PD gait comparisons often emphasized upper-limb or postural tasks or evaluated a limited subset of gait variables, with heterogeneous performance [16], [31], [32], [33], [34]. In contrast, the few wearable-sensor studies that directly compared PSP and PD using gait reported stronger performance [9], [10], [21]. The present study is unique in three respects: (i) we analyzed a relatively large sample of 34 PSP and 410 PD within a single standardized protocol; (ii) we conducted a head-to-head comparison of raw IMU time-series and aggregate spatiotemporal features in the same cohort and pipeline; and (iii) we benchmarked eight classifier families with a prespecified emphasis on tree-based ensembles (XGBoost, random forest) and provided model-agnostic explanations (global SHAP) together with directional clinical mapping (increased double support, terminal double support, and mediolateral sway; reduced stride length, gait speed, and trunk/lumbar range of motion).
However, several limitations should be noted. The single-center retrospective design restricts generalizability. Although within-fold SMOTE and macro-averaged metrics were applied, PSP remained more difficult to detect than PD, underscoring the need to improve sensitivity and reduce false negatives through complementary assessments such as gaze or speech. Because this study was retrospective, the analysis was limited to the iTUG that formed part of the routine clinical assessment. Longer continuous walking protocols (e.g., the 2-minute or 10-m walk) and complementary mobility tasks may capture additional gait characteristics and should be investigated in future prospective studies. Future work will also focus on reproducing the models on alternative IMU systems in prospective, multisite study designs, and extending evaluation beyond iTUG administered in the clinic to home monitoring.
In addition, although all participants were assessed using the same sensors, acquisition protocol, and clinical environment, indoor magnetic disturbances may influence magnetometer-based orientation estimates and therefore represent an inherent limitation of IMU systems incorporating magnetometers.
In this study, we applied the same machine learning models to both raw time-series and aggregated (pre-processed) IMU data to directly compare their performance metrics. The primary objective was to compare the two data representations while keeping the learning framework consistent across modalities. Both pipelines yielded similar discriminatory ability, suggesting that carefully engineered summary features preserve most of the discriminative information contained in the raw signals. We recognize that dedicated sequence-based architectures (e.g., recurrent or convolutional neural networks) may better capture the temporal dependencies inherent in raw signals. However, such architectures also introduce substantially greater model complexity and typically require considerably larger training datasets to achieve robust generalization. Consequently, benchmarking sequence-oriented deep learning models was beyond the scope of the present study. Although our cohort is relatively large from a clinical standpoint, particularly given the challenges of collecting standardized IMU assessments in patients with PD and PSP, the sample size remains modest for training more complex machine learning models. Future work should therefore explore sequence-oriented approaches in larger, multicenter datasets to determine whether they provide incremental benefit over clinically interpretable aggregate-feature approaches.
5. Conclusions
Wearable-sensor gait analysis combined with ensemble machine learning differentiates PSP from PD with robust accuracy (macro-F1 ≈ 0.87–0.88) and yields clinically interpretable gait signatures consistent with known pathophysiology. The close agreement between raw and aggregate inputs suggests multiple viable pathways for clinical translation, pending prospective multi-site validation and targeted optimization for PSP detection.
